**Theorem 6.3 (unconditional $1/99$ with actual prehistory priors).**
Use the same preparation and six-unit histories of Proposition 3.1 for
the heights in (AC.20). Leave their prefixes unchanged, retain all first
failures, and apply any common specimen-only procedure only at the reached
live cut. For at least one of the twelve deterministic histories the full
prefix-plus-continuation error is at least $1/99$.

Proof. Formula (AC.6) gives the exact live probabilities

$$
q_-=\frac{32}{177},\qquad q_0=\frac29,\qquad
q_+=\frac{32}{111}.
\tag{AC.22}
$$

Consider two mathematical source experiments, each with the uniform four
nuisance choices. The first chooses $x_0$ before preparation. The second
chooses $x_-$ and $x_+$ before preparation with probabilities
$p_-=59/96$ and $p_+=37/96$. These are priors for the actual same
preparation, not posterior weights silently substituted for source priors.
Both are realized within one common generator: an unread fair classical
selector chooses the middle case or the stated outer prior before the
six-unit history begins. The two experiments are its conditional laws;
neither that selector nor its height/nuisance choices assists the processor.
They satisfy

$$
p_-q_-=p_+q_+=1/9,\qquad
p_-q_-+p_+q_+=q_0=2/9.
\tag{AC.23}
$$

Thus both experiments reach the cut with live mass $2/9$ and the same
normalized accessible density. Their earlier stopped archives need not
coincide. Each archive is exactly retained in its own true/simulated
comparison and cannot send information to another run's live branch.

For each deterministic history let $E_{x,\beta,m}$ be its unconditional
error. The unchanged prefixes and absorbing branches give
$E_{x,\beta,m}=q_x e_{x,\beta,m}$ in the declared reduced output task;
if further fields are required, the corresponding full error is at least
this. Multiply (AC.19) by $2/9$ and use (AC.23):

$$
\tfrac29\bar e_0+
 p_-q_-\bar e_-+p_+q_+\bar e_+
\geq\tfrac29D=\frac2{99}.
\tag{AC.24}
$$

The left side is the sum of the two source-prior averages of deterministic
unconditional errors. At least one average is at least $1/99$, hence at
least one deterministic error is at least $1/99$. Earlier failures were
neither equated across experiments nor discarded. $\square$

The entire seven-unit rational witness has support at least $5/256$,
height speed at most $27/256$, phase speed at most $8\pi$ and phase
variation at most $16\pi$. For a piecewise constant height-velocity
representative its variation on the open execution interval, with one-sided
endpoint traces and no exterior extension by zero, is at most $31/64$.
Indeed its velocities are
$(0,-16,0,16,0,x\cdot256-32,6)/256$; their jumps total at most
$(64+27+33)/256=31/64$. The finite witness needs no near-wall limit,
unbounded winding, arbitrarily rapid oscillation or infinite programme
description.

